[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known, with one fix queued. Read back, the reports under
it are not one bug. They are three, and two of them have no ticket, no owner and no fix.

The desk did not miss this. The kb has been showing it on every build.

## What the ticket already shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table splits the ticket by [`causeOf`](/kb/triage/dedupe), and `causes.ts` says in as many
words that the second one is "a different bug". The last column says it has no ticket of its
own. The footnote under the table counts reports the desk read as something else again.

<Callout kind="warn">
Nothing here is new data. This is the same table the incident page renders, read as a finding
instead of a detail.
</Callout>

## The third problem

`causeOf` decides from the tenant and the channel. It never reads the report. So a report
describing a different symptom still comes out as `parser`, because its tenant writes dates
day first — which is true, and beside the point.

<OneDayMove ticket={4471} />

These are not a swapped month. A swap moves 7 April to 4 July. These move one day, or move a
meeting between two cities, or deliver an invite before the booking that caused it. The month
is right in every one of them.

## The three tickets

| problem | reports | who sees it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The shared parser reads every date month first, so day-first tenants get the month and the day exchanged. | 374 | every tenant, all three channels |
| `booking-v3` prints the hint `MM/DD/YYYY`, people type month first, and the form reads it day first. | 33 | three tenants, the form only |
| A one-day move, unrelated to date order. | 5 | five tenants, app and email |

374 + 33 + 5 = 412, the whole ticket.

The 5 sit inside the 379 that `causeOf` calls `parser`, because it files them by tenant. Counted
as their own problem they come out of that group, which is why the parser row here reads 374 and
the table above reads 379.

## Why one ticket held three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone, so
nobody has to read the body. Every report here has a subject like "Date is wrong" or "Wrong day
on the invite". Twelve subjects cover all 412, and they describe the symptom a person noticed,
never the bug underneath.

The decision names this consequence and accepts it: "Reports with different subjects about the
same bug end up in separate tickets." The ticket shows the opposite too, and that one is not
written down: reports with the *same* subject about *different* bugs end up in one ticket, and
the count makes it look settled.

A count is evidence of how many people are affected. It is not evidence of how many things are
broken.

## What the closing left behind

#4471 was closed on 5 October. The engineering update gives two outcomes:

| cause | outcome | what changed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | fixed | `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` |
| `hint` | won't fix | `booking-v3` form retired |

The one-day move is in neither row. It was not fixed, not declined, and not named. It was
closed along with the ticket it had been merged into.

That is the same move made twice. The merge put five reports under a cause that did not
describe them, and the close carried them out again. Neither step was a decision about the
bug. Both were decisions about the ticket.

The parser fix cannot have reached them: it changes how a date string is read, and these five
have the month right. What they describe — one day, one month affected, a meeting split
between two cities, an invite that predates its own booking — sits in time handling, which
nobody has looked at.

<Callout kind="warn">
Two of the three are settled. The third has no fix, no decision and no owner, and the ticket
that was holding it is now closed.
</Callout>

## What to do

- Keep a ticket open for the one-day move. It is the only one of the three still live, and it
  is the one with nobody's name on it.
- `hint` closed as won't fix, not as fixed. The form was retired, so the surface is gone; the
  reader that exchanged the date was never corrected. If any form is ever read that way again,
  these 33 reports come back.
- `causeOf` decides from the tenant and the channel and never reads the report. Until that
  changes, any symptom it has no cause for will land in whichever cause the tenant implies,
  and will be counted there.
- `intakeTag` disagrees with `causeOf` on 38 of the 412. Intake guesses from the reporter's
  browser; the cause depends on the tenant. Worth its own page, not this one.
- The export ships 427 rows for 412 reports; the pages overlap. `uniqueById` already handles
  it. Any count taken straight off the export is 15 too high.

